From $26,753 to $45,370 the rate is 29%, higher than the 20.5% before it. BC tax starts here, and the BC tax reduction, which lowers your BC tax, shrinks by 3.56 cents for every dollar of income above $25,570. So more of each extra dollar goes to BC tax. The reduction is gone at about $45,371 of salary.
Once the reduction is gone, the rate falls to 25.5%. It is 28% from $50,837 (the next BC bracket) and 34% from $59,079 (the federal 20.5% bracket).
Then it falls as Employment Insurance and the Canada Pension Plan stop: 33% at $68,900, 31% at $74,600 and 28% at $85,000. After that it climbs to a top rate of 53.5%, where the line is straight.
From $266,672 of salary the line is straight: the rate stops rising, and every extra dollar loses the top rate, 53.5%."
`;

exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-mb 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $68,900, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $74,600. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $401,127 up, the line is straight: you keep 48.75% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 55.57% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. Employment Insurance starts just above $2,000 and counts for more as your salary rises, until the full rate applies by about $2,033. The average is about 1.6% from about $2,034.
Up to about $17,403 you pay no income tax, only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. The average is 5.5% at $10,000.
After that the average climbs: 15.9% at $30,000, 21.9% at $50,000, 25.9% at $70,000 and 28.6% at $100,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 44.4%, while the top marginal rate is 51.5%.
Manitoba’s own tax starts before federal tax does, so the average is 8.5% at $20,000, more than in provinces whose own tax starts later.

marginalRate
Below $2,000 the rate is 0%. From $2,000 to about $2,033 the rate is 100%, because the Employment Insurance counted rises by $1 for every $1 of salary, until it reaches about $33. After that it takes 1.63 cents of each extra dollar.
From $4,000 to about $17,403 the rate is 7.5%: Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, with no income tax yet.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.5 cents of each dollar. Employment Insurance stops at about $68,900, and the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600. A second Canada Pension Plan tier takes 4 cents of each dollar from $74,600 to $85,000, so the rate falls less at $74,600 than at $85,000.
Manitoba’s own tax starts before the federal tax does. The rate is 18.5% from $17,403: Manitoba tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. It is 31.5% from $19,201, once federal tax joins in, then 30.5% from $23,141. The Manitoba Family Tax Benefit lowers your Manitoba tax and shrinks by 9 cents for every dollar of income. It is gone at about $23,141, so the rate falls by about 1 point there.
It steps up to 32.5% at $47,440 (Manitoba’s next bracket) and 39% at $59,079 (the federal 20.5% bracket), then falls as Employment Insurance and the Canada Pension Plan stop: 37.5% at $68,900, 36% at $74,600 and 33.5% at $85,000.
At $201,128 of salary it steps up about 1 point to 47.5%, and it reaches 51.3% from $259,609 to $401,126. Then it drops about 1 point, to 50.4%. The cause is the Manitoba basic personal amount, which shrinks to zero as net income goes from $200,000 to $400,000, so you lose a little tax saving on each extra dollar. The line is straight from $401,127."
`;

