From $25,101 to $30,763 the rate is 44.5%, much higher than before. The reduction shrinks by 16 cents for every dollar of income above $24,191, so you start paying the provincial tax and lose the reduction at the same time. It is gone at about $30,764.
Once the reduction is gone the rate falls to 28.5% from $30,764. It steps up to 34.5% from $45,094 (the second bracket) and 40.5% from $59,079 (the federal 20.5% bracket), then down as pension and insurance stop. The top rate in this chart is 54%, from $286,446, where the line is straight. The form adds higher rates above about $564,000."
`;

