exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-on 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $68,900, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $74,600. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $259,609 up, the line is straight: you keep 46.47% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 54.62% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. Employment Insurance starts just above $2,000 and counts for more as your salary rises, until the full rate applies by about $2,033. The average is about 1.6% from about $2,034.
Up to about $19,201 you pay no income tax, only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. The average is 5.5% at $10,000 and 7.1% at $20,000.
After that the average climbs: 12.5% at $30,000, 19.7% at $50,000, 23.2% at $70,000 and 25.8% at $100,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 45.4%, while the top marginal rate is 53.5%.

marginalRate
Below $2,000 the rate is 0%. From $2,000 to about $2,033 the rate is 100%, because the Employment Insurance counted rises by $1 for every $1 of salary, until it reaches about $33. After that it takes 1.63 cents of each extra dollar.
From $4,000 to about $19,201 the rate is 7.5%: Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, with no income tax yet. It is 20.5% from $19,201, when federal income tax starts.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.5 cents of each dollar. Employment Insurance stops at about $68,900, and the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600. A second Canada Pension Plan tier takes 4 cents of each dollar from $74,600 to $85,000, so the rate falls less at $74,600 than at $85,000.
From $20,167 to $25,217 of salary the Ontario Health Premium adds 6 cents for every dollar, up to $300 in all. The rate is 26.5%, then 20.5% again.
From $32,794 to $38,853 the rate is 30% or more, higher than the 20.5% before it. The Ontario Low-income Individuals and Families Tax (LIFT) Credit lowers your Ontario tax. It shrinks by 5 cents for every dollar above $32,500, so more of each extra dollar goes to Ontario tax.
From $36,000 to $38,500 of taxable income the Ontario Health Premium adds 6 cents more, lifting the rate to 36%. LIFT keeps shrinking until about $50,470, and the rate falls to 25% once it is gone.
Three short spikes push the rate above the top rate of 53.5%: 54.9% from $48,450 to $49,055, 59.1% from $72,692 to $73,297, and 73.3% from $201,127 to $201,727 of salary. Each one is a step in the Ontario Health Premium. Across a band about $600 wide, the premium grows by 25 cents for every extra dollar, adding $150 in all. The rate falls back as soon as the band ends.
Ontario’s surtax raises the rate twice. A surtax is an extra tax on top of your Ontario tax, charged once that tax passes a set amount. It adds 20 cents for every dollar of Ontario tax above $5,818, and another 36 cents above $7,446. The rate rises to 31.5% at $98,591, and to 38% at $115,038.
From $259,609 of salary the line is straight: the rate stops rising, and every extra dollar loses the top rate, 53.5%."
`;

exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-qc 1`] = `
"takeHome
Québec Pension Plan, Employment Insurance and QPIP stop growing at different salaries. Employment Insurance stops at $68,900, at most $895.70 a year. The main QPP amount stops at $74,600, and its second tier at $85,000. QPIP stops at $103,000, at most $442.90.
From $259,609 up, the line is straight: you keep 46.7% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 52.96% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. At $2,000 QPIP starts, and the average is 0.4%. Employment Insurance counts from just above $2,000.
The average is 5.8% at $10,000, 7.3% at $20,000 and 18.1% at $30,000. It passes 23.9% at $50,000, 28.2% at $70,000 and 31.2% at $100,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 47%, while the top marginal rate is 53.3%.

marginalRate
Below $2,000 the rate is 0%. At $2,000 QPIP starts and its whole $8.60 counts at once. From $2,000 to about $2,026 the rate is about 100%, because the Employment Insurance counted rises by $1 for every $1 of salary, until it reaches $26. After that Employment Insurance and QPIP take 1.73 cents of each extra dollar.
From $3,500 the rate is 8%: Québec Pension Plan, Employment Insurance and QPIP, with no income tax yet. The QPP payment starts above $3,500.
It is 18.8% from $19,281, when federal income tax starts, and 31.8% from $20,341, when Québec income tax starts.
From $21,350 to $29,882 the rate is 39.1%, then 40.4% from $24,167 and 44.3% from $26,571. Two things cause this. The drug insurance premium grows by 7.84 cents for every dollar of income, then 11.76 cents, until it reaches its maximum at $29,882. And the deduction for workers stops growing at $24,167. Then the rate falls to 32.6%.
It stays 32.6% until $56,324. It is 37.6% from $56,324 (Québec’s second bracket) and 43% from $59,079 (the federal 20.5% bracket).
Then it falls as payments stop: Employment Insurance at $68,900, the main QPP amount at $74,600 (a second QPP tier takes 4 cents of each dollar from there to $85,000), and QPIP at $103,000.
After that it only climbs: 41.1% from $111,257 (Québec’s third bracket), 45.7% from $118,172 (the federal 26% bracket), 47.5% from $134,822 (Québec’s top bracket), 50.2% from $182,567 (the federal 29% bracket), and 53.3% from $259,609, where the line is straight."
`;

